WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.316
There is a part under some workbenches that you will never notice, until it is missing.

00:00:07.596 --> 00:00:09.462
It is called the top support rail.

00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:12.710
A worktop is strong, but over a long span,

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:16.366
steady weight and vibration can make it bow in the middle.

00:00:16.646 --> 00:00:21.348
Once a top bends, precision work becomes harder and the surface wears faster.

00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:27.003
A top support rail is a pair of steel rails that run front and back under the worktop,

00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:28.238
between the supports.

00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:33.530
They stiffen the top, spread the load to the legs, and resist flexing from grinders,

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:34.962
presses and heavy parts.

00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:41.061
On Furnisys Scitec benches with at least one leg frame, top support rails are required.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:47.415
On Pro benches, a rail is added where supports are more than 1000 millimetres apart,

00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:51.103
and tops 84 inches and wider also get an extra leg.

00:00:51.703 --> 00:00:55.676
So if your bench is long or carries a machine, keep that rail in place.

00:00:55.956 --> 00:00:57.701
Have you ever seen a sagging bench?

00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:01.998
Comment below, and next, learn how levelling glides keep it steady.

00:01:02.598 --> 00:01:06.843
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

